 unanimously call it Solid or Safe D and markets price Moore around 96-98 percent.", "watch": "A poor October debate performance by Moore or an abrupt national shift against his party could narrow the margin, though Cox would need a historic swing to threaten the outcome.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 51 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Wes Moore (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Maryland remains one of the most Democratic-leaning states in the country, and incumbent Wes Moore enters the general election with every structural advantage. He defeated Dan Cox by roughly 32 points in their 2022 matchup, and the two are facing off again. Moore has raised about $15.5 million and holds $6.5 million on hand, while Cox has raised roughly $242,000. The only recent poll of the race, from August, showed Moore ahead 58-31 among likely voters.\n\nAll six major race raters call the contest Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets price Moore's win probability around 96-98 percent. Cox has campaigned on electricity costs and data-center issues, and the candidates are set to debate in October, but no public evidence suggests a competitive race. Moore is positioned to win comfortably; the main uncertainty is the size of the margin rather than the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 19.52}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 20.0}]}